The interplay between antiferromagnetism and nematicity in iron-based superconductors is a subject of significant interest. Although both phenomena are present in BaFe$_2$As$_2$, the coexistence of nematicity and superconductivity in LiFeAs remains controversial. This study examines the interplay between nematicity and superconductivity in LiFeAs using a self-consistent self-energy-based mean-field theory. By incorporating a breathing mode spin fluctuation, we observe that superconductivity emerges without triggering a nematic instability. These findings support the presence of nematic fluctuations in LiFeAs and suggest that nematicity could be induced through doping or pressure. Furthermore, the calculated superconducting gap structure aligns with experimental data, reinforcing the validity of our approach. Finally, the study explores the effects of hole doping and Fermi surface reconstruction on the superconducting gap structure. These results indicate that the superconducting gap structure in LiFeAs is highly sensitive to doping levels, offering a potential explanation for the observed evolution of the gap structure with varying doping. Additionally, the evolution of the gap structure with pressure is qualitatively accounted for by the model. Overall, this research provides a comprehensive understanding of the interplay between nematicity and superconductivity in LiFeAs. The model code is publicly accessible at \href{https://github.com/ChenYi1992/Nematic-Fluctuation}{github.com/ChenYi1992/Nematic-Fluctuation}. \end{document}
